1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,663
In nineteen sixty-three, the United States Army was losing

2
00:00:03,663 --> 00:00:07,733
space inside a city it had built beneath Greenland's ice.

3
00:00:07,733 --> 00:00:10,989
Not to an enemy. Not to an avalanche.

4
00:00:10,989 --> 00:00:13,838
The ceiling itself was slowly coming down.

5
00:00:13,838 --> 00:00:17,501
This was Camp Century: a working settlement hidden beneath

6
00:00:17,501 --> 00:00:20,350
the surface of the Greenland ice sheet.

7
00:00:20,350 --> 00:00:24,013
There were sleeping quarters, workshops, medical facilities, heated corridors,

8
00:00:24,013 --> 00:00:27,676
and a nuclear power plant. Tracked vehicles could travel

9
00:00:27,676 --> 00:00:31,339
along an underground main street. But the Army's own

10
00:00:31,339 --> 00:00:35,002
measurements told another story. The Army documented one section

11
00:00:35,002 --> 00:00:38,665
of its main passage losing roughly twelve percent of

12
00:00:38,665 --> 00:00:42,328
its cross-sectional area per year. In a separate side

13
00:00:42,328 --> 00:00:45,991
passage, the figure was seventeen percent. Those were measured

14
00:00:45,991 --> 00:00:48,840
examples, not identical rates for every tunnel.

15
00:00:49,140 --> 00:00:52,614
The Army's engineers estimated that keeping this one camp's

16
00:00:52,614 --> 00:00:56,087
tunnels at their original dimensions would require cutting and

17
00:00:56,087 --> 00:00:59,947
removing around twenty thousand cubic yards of snow every year.

18
00:00:59,947 --> 00:01:04,192
That was an estimate, not a record of snow actually removed.

19
00:01:04,192 --> 00:01:07,666
The Army's surviving film records the machines, trenches, and

20
00:01:07,666 --> 00:01:11,140
extraordinary construction effort. The published engineering report shows how

21
00:01:11,140 --> 00:01:14,613
the arches and rooms were assembled beneath the surface.

22
00:01:14,613 --> 00:01:18,087
Everything looks finished. But the biggest construction job might

23
00:01:18,087 --> 00:01:20,789
have been the one that never ended.

24
00:01:20,789 --> 00:01:24,262
Now for the part the public movie did not

25
00:01:24,262 --> 00:01:25,420
tell in full.

26
00:01:25,880 --> 00:01:29,135
As the research program developed, military planners proposed a

27
00:01:29,135 --> 00:01:32,390
much larger system: buried railways and launching locations for

28
00:01:32,390 --> 00:01:35,646
hundreds of nuclear-armed missiles. The arsenal remained a proposal.

29
00:01:35,646 --> 00:01:38,901
The base was real. The mystery isn't whether the

30
00:01:38,901 --> 00:01:42,156
Army could build beneath the ice. It clearly could.

31
00:01:42,156 --> 00:01:45,411
The question is why building one working city did

32
00:01:45,411 --> 00:01:49,028
not lead to the enormous underground military system being imagined.

33
00:01:49,028 --> 00:01:52,283
And the first clue lies in a journey of

34
00:01:52,283 --> 00:01:55,900
one hundred thirty-eight miles, through a place without a road.

35
00:01:56,740 --> 00:01:59,865
The year is nineteen fifty-nine. On the northwest Greenland

36
00:01:59,865 --> 00:02:02,990
ice sheet, there is no construction site as most

37
00:02:02,990 --> 00:02:06,115
people would recognize one. There is no paved roadway

38
00:02:06,115 --> 00:02:09,935
leading to a construction gate. The buildings do not exist yet.

39
00:02:09,935 --> 00:02:13,060
There is no paved road leading to the site.

40
00:02:13,060 --> 00:02:16,185
Greenland is part of the Danish realm, and the

41
00:02:16,185 --> 00:02:19,310
United States operates in the region under defense arrangements.

42
00:02:19,310 --> 00:02:22,435
Geography, however, has its own rules. The project uses

43
00:02:22,435 --> 00:02:26,255
Camp Tuto, a staging area near the edge of the ice.

44
00:02:26,255 --> 00:02:29,380
From there, men and machinery must travel far inland.

45
00:02:29,740 --> 00:02:33,837
The distance to Camp Century is approximately one hundred thirty-eight miles.

46
00:02:33,837 --> 00:02:37,189
Tractors pull long sled trains across the snow, carrying

47
00:02:37,189 --> 00:02:40,168
the materials needed to create an underground settlement.

48
00:02:40,168 --> 00:02:43,520
Some loads are so awkward that the Army has

49
00:02:43,520 --> 00:02:46,872
to make special sleds for them. It is the

50
00:02:46,872 --> 00:02:49,851
image that makes the construction story so tangible:

51
00:02:49,851 --> 00:02:53,203
a small line of heavy machines moving across an

52
00:02:53,203 --> 00:02:56,555
almost featureless white horizon. But the cargo tells you

53
00:02:56,555 --> 00:02:59,907
what is really happening. Steel, timber, prefabricated rooms, fuel,

54
00:02:59,907 --> 00:03:03,258
and heavy electrical equipment. In the first season, roughly

55
00:03:03,258 --> 00:03:06,610
one thousand short tons of materials move from Camp

56
00:03:06,610 --> 00:03:08,100
Tuto toward the site.

57
00:03:08,660 --> 00:03:12,351
In nineteen sixty, the total is another four thousand six hundred.

58
00:03:12,351 --> 00:03:16,042
About five thousand six hundred short tons in two construction seasons.

59
00:03:16,042 --> 00:03:19,733
And every machine hauling that cargo needs fuel of its own.

60
00:03:19,733 --> 00:03:22,417
Spare parts have to follow the same route.

61
00:03:22,417 --> 00:03:25,437
When conditions change, a trip can become another test

62
00:03:25,437 --> 00:03:28,456
of the transport system. This is the first hidden

63
00:03:28,456 --> 00:03:31,476
cost of building in the Arctic: before your machines

64
00:03:31,476 --> 00:03:34,496
can solve the problem, you must build a way

65
00:03:34,496 --> 00:03:37,516
to bring the machines there. Yet by October nineteen

66
00:03:37,516 --> 00:03:40,200
sixty, the Army has created a functioning camp.

67
00:03:40,880 --> 00:03:43,694
What came next was even stranger. They did not

68
00:03:43,694 --> 00:03:46,508
blast their way through a mountain. They did not

69
00:03:46,508 --> 00:03:49,010
drill an enormous tunnel through solid glacial ice.

70
00:03:49,010 --> 00:03:52,137
They took advantage of something easier to cut than rock.

71
00:03:52,137 --> 00:03:53,700
And much harder to trust.

72
00:03:54,600 --> 00:03:57,972
The central construction machine at Camp Century was called

73
00:03:57,972 --> 00:04:01,343
the Peter snow miller. In the Army's period images,

74
00:04:01,343 --> 00:04:04,715
this machine resembles an industrial cutter trying to eat

75
00:04:04,715 --> 00:04:07,337
its way through an endless white wall.

76
00:04:07,337 --> 00:04:10,334
It cuts a long trench into compacted snow.

77
00:04:10,334 --> 00:04:13,706
The next step is deceptively simple: build inside the

78
00:04:13,706 --> 00:04:17,077
trench, cover it with a roof, then put snow

79
00:04:17,077 --> 00:04:19,700
back on top. This is cut-and-cover construction.

80
00:04:19,700 --> 00:04:23,071
But unlike an ordinary city, the material above and

81
00:04:23,071 --> 00:04:25,694
beside the buildings is not stable earth.

82
00:04:25,694 --> 00:04:29,440
It's snow and firn, constantly responding to pressure and temperature.

83
00:04:29,960 --> 00:04:32,476
The Army tested different forms of roof.

84
00:04:32,476 --> 00:04:35,710
In some locations, the trench walls were undercut so

85
00:04:35,710 --> 00:04:38,226
that compacted snow could form an arch.

86
00:04:38,226 --> 00:04:41,461
In others, metal arches helped support the larger openings.

87
00:04:41,461 --> 00:04:44,695
The engineers were trying to make the surrounding snow

88
00:04:44,695 --> 00:04:48,649
part of the structure, without allowing it to destroy the structure.

89
00:04:48,649 --> 00:04:51,883
For the broad reactor building space, the Army needed

90
00:04:51,883 --> 00:04:55,118
a trench around forty feet wide. That width brought

91
00:04:55,118 --> 00:04:58,353
new structural loads and new risks. Some of the

92
00:04:58,353 --> 00:05:01,587
first failures were quietly revealing. In certain sections, temporary

93
00:05:01,587 --> 00:05:04,822
roof forms were removed before the snow above them

94
00:05:04,822 --> 00:05:05,900
had properly strengthened.

95
00:05:06,660 --> 00:05:09,908
The openings began to deform. The solution was sometimes

96
00:05:09,908 --> 00:05:12,795
to leave the support forms where they were.

97
00:05:12,795 --> 00:05:16,764
These compromises would matter long after the promotional film was finished.

98
00:05:16,764 --> 00:05:19,290
But the buildings were only the beginning.

99
00:05:19,290 --> 00:05:22,538
Under the snow, Camp Century developed a main street

100
00:05:22,538 --> 00:05:25,786
and a network of smaller passages. The layout included

101
00:05:25,786 --> 00:05:29,034
a dining hall, barracks, a medical facility, workshops, storage,

102
00:05:29,034 --> 00:05:31,560
recreational rooms, even a chapel and theater.

103
00:05:31,560 --> 00:05:35,530
The camp was designed for as many as two hundred people.

104
00:05:35,530 --> 00:05:38,417
From the outside, a blank field of snow.

105
00:05:38,417 --> 00:05:39,860
Inside, an operating settlement.

106
00:05:40,400 --> 00:05:43,079
There was one important compromise. Every roof carried the

107
00:05:43,079 --> 00:05:45,758
weight of snow above it. And the camp would

108
00:05:45,758 --> 00:05:49,032
be adding something that made the surrounding environment even more complicated.

109
00:05:49,032 --> 00:05:51,711
Heat. Not just from the people living there, but

110
00:05:51,711 --> 00:05:54,389
from a machine that cost more than most of

111
00:05:54,389 --> 00:05:55,580
the city around it.

112
00:05:56,380 --> 00:06:00,125
The Army had a supply problem it could not escape.

113
00:06:00,125 --> 00:06:03,495
Keep a remote settlement warm with diesel generators, and

114
00:06:03,495 --> 00:06:06,490
every season brings another long convoy carrying fuel.

115
00:06:06,490 --> 00:06:09,860
Or install a machine that can produce a large

116
00:06:09,860 --> 00:06:13,231
amount of power without the same continuous fuel deliveries.

117
00:06:13,231 --> 00:06:17,350
That second idea became PM-two-A, Camp Century's portable nuclear power plant.

118
00:06:17,350 --> 00:06:20,720
It wasn't a bomb. It was an electrical and

119
00:06:20,720 --> 00:06:24,090
heating system for the camp. The price was extraordinary.

120
00:06:24,090 --> 00:06:27,460
A nineteen sixty-five Army report gives Camp Century's actual

121
00:06:27,460 --> 00:06:30,830
construction cost as seven point nine two million dollars.

122
00:06:31,180 --> 00:06:34,283
The nuclear power plant alone accounted for about five

123
00:06:34,283 --> 00:06:36,696
point seven million. Those are period dollars.

124
00:06:36,696 --> 00:06:39,799
And they mean that the plant consumed the great

125
00:06:39,799 --> 00:06:42,901
majority of the initial project cost. The reactor arrived

126
00:06:42,901 --> 00:06:46,349
as another set of heavy loads hauled across the ice.

127
00:06:46,349 --> 00:06:50,141
Engineers had to assemble it in the prepared underground plant space.

128
00:06:50,141 --> 00:06:53,244
Its reactor produced heat on the scale of ten

129
00:06:53,244 --> 00:06:56,346
megawatts, while the electrical output supplied to the camp

130
00:06:56,346 --> 00:06:58,759
was roughly one and a half megawatts.

131
00:06:58,759 --> 00:07:01,862
Those figures measure different things. Reactor heat is not

132
00:07:01,862 --> 00:07:04,620
the same as electricity delivered to the buildings.

133
00:07:05,060 --> 00:07:08,018
At Camp Century, the heat itself was useful.

134
00:07:08,018 --> 00:07:11,345
The base required water. Engineers developed systems to melt

135
00:07:11,345 --> 00:07:15,412
snow and ice and recover usable water from beneath the surface.

136
00:07:15,412 --> 00:07:18,740
But this creates another problem. Where do you put

137
00:07:18,740 --> 00:07:21,328
the water after people have used it?

138
00:07:21,328 --> 00:07:23,916
The Army's engineers built underground disposal sumps.

139
00:07:23,916 --> 00:07:27,243
One was placed too near living quarters during a

140
00:07:27,243 --> 00:07:31,310
rush to finish before winter. The odors reached the occupied buildings.

141
00:07:31,310 --> 00:07:34,638
And that wasn't the worst consequence. Warm liquid moving

142
00:07:34,638 --> 00:07:37,226
into the snow changed the ground nearby.

143
00:07:37,226 --> 00:07:40,554
The original report described the floor near the disposal

144
00:07:40,554 --> 00:07:43,881
area settling by roughly four feet, forcing the removal

145
00:07:43,881 --> 00:07:45,360
of two nearby buildings.

146
00:07:45,360 --> 00:07:48,908
The camp had literally altered the material holding up

147
00:07:48,908 --> 00:07:52,456
part of its own infrastructure. Meanwhile, the nuclear plant's

148
00:07:52,456 --> 00:07:56,004
turbine-generator developed repeated mechanical troubles. Keeping the city supplied

149
00:07:56,004 --> 00:07:59,947
meant solving ordinary industrial failures in a profoundly unusual place.

150
00:07:59,947 --> 00:08:02,706
The reactor was a real technical achievement.

151
00:08:02,706 --> 00:08:06,254
But the more systems the Army put underground, the

152
00:08:06,254 --> 00:08:09,803
more there was to maintain. The reactor had been

153
00:08:09,803 --> 00:08:12,562
planned before Project Iceworm was formally proposed.

154
00:08:12,562 --> 00:08:16,110
Its cost cannot be treated as proof that the

155
00:08:16,110 --> 00:08:19,658
later missile plan financed the camp. But the working

156
00:08:19,658 --> 00:08:22,812
research camp made an extraordinary idea seem tangible:

157
00:08:22,812 --> 00:08:26,360
use buried infrastructure to conceal launching positions that would

158
00:08:26,360 --> 00:08:29,120
not always stay in the same place.

159
00:08:30,030 --> 00:08:32,685
The Army made films about Camp Century.

160
00:08:32,685 --> 00:08:36,098
The cameras showed construction equipment, comfortable rooms, Arctic research,

161
00:08:36,098 --> 00:08:39,890
and an American engineering triumph. Much of that was true.

162
00:08:39,890 --> 00:08:43,303
The camp was real. The scientific work was real.

163
00:08:43,303 --> 00:08:46,716
The challenges of living beneath the ice were real.

164
00:08:46,716 --> 00:08:50,129
But military research connected it to another proposal that

165
00:08:50,129 --> 00:08:52,783
the public film did not fully explain.

166
00:08:52,783 --> 00:08:56,196
Project Iceworm. The idea was to use Greenland's ice

167
00:08:56,196 --> 00:09:00,368
sheet to conceal a vast network of mobile ballistic missile positions.

168
00:09:00,368 --> 00:09:03,022
A fixed missile base can be mapped.

169
00:09:03,022 --> 00:09:06,435
A missile moved through an underground network would be

170
00:09:06,435 --> 00:09:09,090
harder to locate, at least in theory.

171
00:09:10,050 --> 00:09:13,801
Historical research describes plans for roughly six hundred missiles

172
00:09:13,801 --> 00:09:17,134
and more than two thousand possible launching positions.

173
00:09:17,134 --> 00:09:20,468
Not six hundred missiles actually delivered to Greenland.

174
00:09:20,468 --> 00:09:25,052
Not two thousand completed underground silos. Those numbers describe a proposal.

175
00:09:25,052 --> 00:09:28,803
Historical research describes a proposed network across approximately one

176
00:09:28,803 --> 00:09:32,554
hundred thirty-five thousand square kilometers. And the Army had

177
00:09:32,554 --> 00:09:36,721
one experimental component that made the proposal sound less imaginary.

178
00:09:36,721 --> 00:09:40,472
The Army had installed an experimental subsurface railway at

179
00:09:40,472 --> 00:09:44,222
Camp Century, but this was only a research-scale installation.

180
00:09:44,222 --> 00:09:47,973
That was real. But a short experimental track is

181
00:09:47,973 --> 00:09:50,890
not a functioning continent-sized missile transport network.

182
00:09:51,210 --> 00:09:54,103
Every passage would have to remain open.

183
00:09:54,103 --> 00:09:57,823
Every point would require maintenance. Trains, machinery, power, communications,

184
00:09:57,823 --> 00:10:01,543
and crews would all need support. And those demands

185
00:10:01,543 --> 00:10:04,437
would be multiplied thousands of times over.

186
00:10:04,437 --> 00:10:08,157
There was another obstacle. The United States was building

187
00:10:08,157 --> 00:10:11,050
on Greenland, within the Kingdom of Denmark.

188
00:10:11,050 --> 00:10:14,770
An American research installation did not automatically provide political

189
00:10:14,770 --> 00:10:18,077
consent for an enormous hidden nuclear weapons complex.

190
00:10:18,077 --> 00:10:21,797
Within the U.S. military, the Air Force and Navy

191
00:10:21,797 --> 00:10:24,690
were also developing other strategic missile systems.

192
00:10:24,690 --> 00:10:28,410
The Army proposal faced rival technologies, questions about its

193
00:10:28,410 --> 00:10:32,130
military purpose, and problems no engineering demonstration could resolve.

194
00:10:32,570 --> 00:10:35,588
According to a later scientific study, the proposal was

195
00:10:35,588 --> 00:10:38,605
rejected in nineteen sixty-three. The missile network was never

196
00:10:38,605 --> 00:10:40,952
completed, and the weapons were never deployed.

197
00:10:40,952 --> 00:10:43,970
But even if a government had decided to finance

198
00:10:43,970 --> 00:10:47,323
the entire scheme, the base had already produced a warning.

199
00:10:47,323 --> 00:10:50,341
Cutting the tunnels would have been only the beginning.

200
00:10:50,341 --> 00:10:53,358
Keeping a vast network usable would be another challenge,

201
00:10:53,358 --> 00:10:55,370
alongside the military and political objections.

202
00:10:56,320 --> 00:10:59,662
By the early nineteen sixties, Camp Century's engineers were

203
00:10:59,662 --> 00:11:02,261
measuring something their cameras couldn't make glamorous.

204
00:11:02,261 --> 00:11:05,603
The shrinking of empty space. A snow trench can

205
00:11:05,603 --> 00:11:09,317
look perfectly solid. Then, month by month, the roof settles.

206
00:11:09,317 --> 00:11:12,659
The walls creep inward because the snow and firn

207
00:11:12,659 --> 00:11:16,743
around them are deforming under pressure. The Army knew about creep.

208
00:11:16,743 --> 00:11:20,085
This wasn't the discovery of a new physical law.

209
00:11:20,085 --> 00:11:23,427
The problem was the scale of what maintaining a

210
00:11:23,427 --> 00:11:26,027
city inside that moving material actually required.

211
00:11:26,027 --> 00:11:29,740
Look at two diagrams in the Army's nineteen sixty-five report.

212
00:11:30,340 --> 00:11:33,680
They show the same tunnel sections at different dates.

213
00:11:33,680 --> 00:11:37,020
The difference isn't subtle. In the main passage, the

214
00:11:37,020 --> 00:11:40,359
report illustrates one section losing about twelve percent of

215
00:11:40,359 --> 00:11:43,699
its cross-sectional area a year. A side passage is

216
00:11:43,699 --> 00:11:47,039
documented at roughly seventeen percent. The Army also reported

217
00:11:47,039 --> 00:11:50,379
average annual vertical closure of about sixteen inches in

218
00:11:50,379 --> 00:11:54,090
the main passage, and about thirty-four inches in side trenches.

219
00:11:54,090 --> 00:11:57,801
These are clearance losses, not additional percentages of floor area.

220
00:11:57,801 --> 00:12:01,140
Not every trench behaved identically. Heat, geometry, supports, and

221
00:12:01,140 --> 00:12:04,480
snow conditions all mattered. But even the average figures

222
00:12:04,480 --> 00:12:07,820
turn a finished city into a continuing construction site.

223
00:12:07,820 --> 00:12:11,918
A corridor must be tall enough for its occupants and equipment.

224
00:12:11,918 --> 00:12:16,016
When it loses height, someone has to create that space again.

225
00:12:16,016 --> 00:12:19,369
Maintaining the original openings throughout the camp, the Army

226
00:12:19,369 --> 00:12:22,722
estimated, would mean trimming and removing roughly twenty thousand

227
00:12:22,722 --> 00:12:26,075
cubic yards of snow per year. This was a

228
00:12:26,075 --> 00:12:29,428
maintenance estimate, not a claim that twenty thousand cubic

229
00:12:29,428 --> 00:12:32,036
yards were actually hauled out every year.

230
00:12:32,036 --> 00:12:35,389
Still, picture the implication. You build the city once.

231
00:12:35,389 --> 00:12:38,742
Then you assign machinery and crews to keep rebuilding

232
00:12:38,742 --> 00:12:42,840
its empty spaces. And the roof is not the only problem.

233
00:12:42,840 --> 00:12:46,076
Blowing snow buried access ramps. Warm air affected parts

234
00:12:46,076 --> 00:12:50,031
of the surrounding structure. Some forms had been removed too early.

235
00:12:50,031 --> 00:12:53,268
Systems inside the camp produced heat, moisture, and maintenance

236
00:12:53,268 --> 00:12:56,504
demands of their own. Figure thirty-three of the report

237
00:12:56,504 --> 00:12:59,740
shows a soldier using an electric chain saw to

238
00:12:59,740 --> 00:13:02,976
cut snow away from the eave of a building

239
00:13:02,976 --> 00:13:06,212
inside a trench. It looks like an ordinary maintenance

240
00:13:06,212 --> 00:13:09,449
task, until you remember what is above the workers.

241
00:13:09,449 --> 00:13:11,966
More snow, under pressure, settling every season.

242
00:13:11,966 --> 00:13:15,202
It is one thing to accept that burden for

243
00:13:15,202 --> 00:13:16,640
a scientific test camp.

244
00:13:17,220 --> 00:13:19,786
It is quite another to accept it for a

245
00:13:19,786 --> 00:13:22,638
dispersed strategic missile force that must remain ready when needed.

246
00:13:22,638 --> 00:13:25,490
Camp Century demonstrated a way to build in the snow.

247
00:13:25,490 --> 00:13:28,056
Its measurements showed why making that way permanent would

248
00:13:28,056 --> 00:13:30,623
be so hard. And now we reach the part

249
00:13:30,623 --> 00:13:33,189
of the story where the Army had to decide

250
00:13:33,189 --> 00:13:34,900
what the experiment was still worth.

251
00:13:35,730 --> 00:13:39,680
Camp Century did not end in a single cinematic collapse.

252
00:13:39,680 --> 00:13:44,025
Instead, its purpose began to fade. The Iceworm proposal was rejected.

253
00:13:44,025 --> 00:13:47,580
The nuclear power plant was shut down in July

254
00:13:47,580 --> 00:13:51,135
nineteen sixty-three, then dismantled and removed the following year.

255
00:13:51,135 --> 00:13:54,690
One scientific history dates the end of year-round occupation

256
00:13:54,690 --> 00:13:59,035
to summer nineteen sixty-three, while another places it in nineteen sixty-four.

257
00:13:59,035 --> 00:14:01,800
Seasonal work continued into the later sixties;

258
00:14:01,800 --> 00:14:04,960
one study dates final abandonment to nineteen sixty-seven.

259
00:14:04,960 --> 00:14:08,515
Then Camp Century was abandoned. The Army had proved

260
00:14:08,515 --> 00:14:11,675
that an underground Arctic settlement could be constructed.

261
00:14:11,675 --> 00:14:15,230
But the larger weapons system had failed to justify

262
00:14:15,230 --> 00:14:17,600
a path from experiment to deployment.

263
00:14:18,100 --> 00:14:21,559
And shutting down a place under snow is not

264
00:14:21,559 --> 00:14:25,018
the same as making it vanish. The reactor hardware

265
00:14:25,018 --> 00:14:28,477
was taken out. The entire buried infrastructure was not.

266
00:14:28,477 --> 00:14:31,937
Materials, structures, and wastes remained in the ice sheet.

267
00:14:31,937 --> 00:14:35,396
For a long time, the assumption was that snowfall

268
00:14:35,396 --> 00:14:38,470
would continue to seal the abandoned site away.

269
00:14:38,470 --> 00:14:41,930
Decades later, scientists began asking whether that assumption would

270
00:14:41,930 --> 00:14:45,389
remain reliable as Greenland's climate changed. One study in

271
00:14:45,389 --> 00:14:48,848
twenty sixteen modeled the possibility that surface loss of

272
00:14:48,848 --> 00:14:52,307
ice could eventually expose or remobilize buried material under

273
00:14:52,307 --> 00:14:53,460
a high-emissions scenario.

274
00:14:54,000 --> 00:14:57,620
But this was a scenario, not an observation of

275
00:14:57,620 --> 00:15:00,435
a radioactive river running into the sea.

276
00:15:00,435 --> 00:15:04,054
The United States Government Accountability Office reported in twenty

277
00:15:04,054 --> 00:15:07,674
twenty-four that a twenty twenty-one study expected the buried

278
00:15:07,674 --> 00:15:11,696
contamination to remain largely immobile through the year twenty-one hundred.

279
00:15:11,696 --> 00:15:15,315
So the scientific question is not resolved by a

280
00:15:15,315 --> 00:15:18,533
single dramatic headline. Denmark established permanent ice-sheet monitoring.

281
00:15:18,533 --> 00:15:22,554
Different projections depend on their assumptions about Greenland's future climate.

282
00:15:22,554 --> 00:15:26,174
And there is one last twist. The Army went

283
00:15:26,174 --> 00:15:29,793
to Greenland to learn how to build inside ice

284
00:15:29,793 --> 00:15:31,000
that was moving.

285
00:15:31,520 --> 00:15:34,417
When the soldiers left, some of the most valuable

286
00:15:34,417 --> 00:15:37,313
discoveries came not from the buildings they abandoned, but

287
00:15:37,313 --> 00:15:40,854
from what researchers had drilled out of the ground beneath them.

288
00:15:40,854 --> 00:15:43,751
While Camp Century disappeared from view, something much older

289
00:15:43,751 --> 00:15:45,360
was waiting to be found.

290
00:15:46,150 --> 00:15:49,578
In nineteen sixty-six, scientific drilling at Camp Century reached

291
00:15:49,578 --> 00:15:52,244
the bottom of the Greenland ice sheet.

292
00:15:52,244 --> 00:15:55,671
Below more than a thousand meters of ice, researchers

293
00:15:55,671 --> 00:15:58,337
recovered frozen material from the ancient ground.

294
00:15:58,337 --> 00:16:02,527
At the time, Camp Century was already losing its military future.

295
00:16:02,527 --> 00:16:05,954
But the core samples had a future no one

296
00:16:05,954 --> 00:16:09,382
could have fully predicted. The samples were stored and

297
00:16:09,382 --> 00:16:12,810
moved between institutions. Some of the deepest material was

298
00:16:12,810 --> 00:16:16,237
long believed lost. During a twenty nineteen inventory at

299
00:16:16,237 --> 00:16:19,665
the University of Copenhagen, a curator located the long-missing

300
00:16:19,665 --> 00:16:21,950
subglacial samples and their original records.

301
00:16:22,590 --> 00:16:25,892
It was evidence from below the ice sheet itself.

302
00:16:25,892 --> 00:16:29,193
Later studies found traces of ancient plants and other

303
00:16:29,193 --> 00:16:32,495
material associated with environments very different from modern Greenland.

304
00:16:32,495 --> 00:16:35,796
The camp that had been built for a Cold

305
00:16:35,796 --> 00:16:39,098
War engineering experiment had preserved a scientific record of

306
00:16:39,098 --> 00:16:42,400
a far older landscape. Then came a new way

307
00:16:42,400 --> 00:16:45,701
to see the same abandoned structures — this time

308
00:16:45,701 --> 00:16:49,003
from the air. In April twenty twenty-four, a NASA

309
00:16:49,003 --> 00:16:51,938
aircraft was using radar to study Greenland's ice.

310
00:16:51,938 --> 00:16:55,239
The instrument detected the buried structures of Camp Century

311
00:16:55,239 --> 00:16:58,541
in a new level of detail. Earlier radar surveys

312
00:16:58,541 --> 00:17:01,843
had also detected the base, but this instrument revealed

313
00:17:01,843 --> 00:17:03,310
more of its layout.

314
00:17:04,150 --> 00:17:06,889
NASA published the images in November that year.

315
00:17:06,889 --> 00:17:09,628
Not an accessible city filled with working machinery.

316
00:17:09,628 --> 00:17:13,051
Not a secret missile complex still operating beneath the glacier.

317
00:17:13,051 --> 00:17:16,133
NASA reported that the structural remains lay at least

318
00:17:16,133 --> 00:17:19,899
one hundred feet beneath the surface when described in twenty twenty-four.

319
00:17:19,899 --> 00:17:22,980
That is what makes this ending different from the

320
00:17:22,980 --> 00:17:26,061
one in the Army's original film. The Army film

321
00:17:26,061 --> 00:17:29,143
shows how people built a city beneath the snow.

322
00:17:29,143 --> 00:17:32,224
The radar shows what remains after the snow has

323
00:17:32,224 --> 00:17:35,305
continued moving for sixty years. And the scientific core

324
00:17:35,305 --> 00:17:38,386
reveals that the ice also holds records older than

325
00:17:38,386 --> 00:17:41,810
any nation that ever claimed a strategic interest in Greenland.

326
00:17:42,330 --> 00:17:45,217
The engineers built an extraordinary city. They could not

327
00:17:45,217 --> 00:17:47,463
make the material around it stay still.

328
00:17:47,463 --> 00:17:50,350
And the huge missile network for which the experiment

329
00:17:50,350 --> 00:17:52,916
might have served as a model never existed.

330
00:17:52,916 --> 00:17:55,483
The lasting evidence is not a hidden arsenal.

331
00:17:55,483 --> 00:17:58,370
It is the film of the men who built

332
00:17:58,370 --> 00:18:01,257
the place, the measurements showing how the tunnels changed,

333
00:18:01,257 --> 00:18:04,465
and the structures and samples that the ice still holds.

334
00:18:04,465 --> 00:18:07,352
The Army tried to turn Greenland's ice sheet into

335
00:18:07,352 --> 00:18:10,240
an enduring military advantage. What it left behind was

336
00:18:10,240 --> 00:18:13,127
an engineering warning, buried in the very material it

337
00:18:13,127 --> 00:18:14,410
had hoped to master.
